# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only
#
# CAMP-1: the Encamp Fix's report leaves with the camp screen (M5-E1e
# #298, M5-E1f #303, M5-E1g #304) - camp entry, the Fix, the menu's own
# EXIT, and nothing of the seam's left on the adventuring screen after.
# A pair leg, and it can be one: every key reaches the program in both
# halves (`F` on the camp bar is the Fix with the seam on and a letter
# the camp loop ignores with it off). Slot C's party is whole, so the Fix
# declines and the report is `FIX: PARTY HEALED` over `NO HIT POINTS
# RESTORED.`, with no cast, rest or clock in it to move the runs apart.
# Driven by hand on the shorter boot (#291), which the runner cannot yet
# drive: 463 frames compared, 1 of them a dump apart, 0 outside the
# rects. #293 owes every leg here its re-drive.

about the Encamp Fix's report leaves with the camp screen: camp, FIX, EXIT
kind pair
program START.EXE
both code-wheel
seam encamp-fix
until 230000000
every 25

# A repaint lands where it lands: the camp teardown itself is 8 frames
# apart in the two runs. One dump of slack, as every other leg.
slack 1

# Slot C: four strong, whole, and standing where it can camp. ENCAMP once
# the adventuring screen has settled; the Fix once the camp bar is up (the
# camp screen reads its banner off the disk and drains a press that lands
# during the redraw); EXIT once the report has been up a while.
key L@7550
key C@7800
key E@8800
key F@9600
key E@10200

# What the seam owns: the camp screen's own command bar with `FIX` spliced
# in, and rows 0x12..0x16 of the message panel. Row 0x11 is deliberately
# outside `panel` - the frame is handed no title since #298, so that row
# is blank in both runs and this rect says so by leaving it out.
rect bar 0,192,319,199
rect panel 8,144,311,183

# Not the seam's drawing, named rather than hidden. `knot` is the corner
# tile of the viewport box at column 0x10 of row 0x10, which the report's
# frame paints over with a plain edge tile. `banner` is the 3D inset,
# where the camp picture's fire animates on the program's clock: the
# seam's batches shift the program by a few frames, so the two runs' flames
# fall out of phase by more than one dump and it is masked.
rect knot 128,128,135,135
rect banner 24,24,111,111

# Up to the camp bar, not one pixel. The mode word becomes camp at frame
# 8,991 and the camp screen's bar is up by 9,450.
allow 0-9425 none

# The spliced bar, before the Fix is chosen.
allow 9450-9625 bar banner

# The report, on the pass after the command declined, until the exit. The
# knot is allowed here and only here: one still, 9,650, lands between the
# frame's top edge and the border that puts the knot back, both of them
# calls in the one batch (#303).
allow 9650-10225 bar panel knot banner

# Out of camp. The mode word leaves camp at 10,227 (10,219 with the seam
# off) and nothing of the box is on the screen after it. These two dumps
# are before the adventuring view replaces the camp picture, and the inset
# they hold is one the seam-off run holds too, at 10,100 and 10,200.
allow 10250-10275 banner

# The bar is drawn a piece at a time and 8 frames later in the seam-on run,
# so this one dump catches a different number of characters in each.
allow 10300-10300 bar

# From here to the end, not a pixel - the highlight included. It is one
# data-segment byte every bar numbers against its own groups and the
# spliced bar has one group more, so the seam puts it back into the
# program's numbering on the way out of camp (M5-E1g #304).
allow 10325-end none
